from pathlib import Path
import re, shutil, hashlib

src=Path('latex_paper/manuscript.tex')
original=src.read_text(encoding='utf-8')
s=original
changes=[]
def paragraph(start,new):
    global s
    a=s.index(start)
    b=s.find('\n\n',a)
    boundaries=[m.start()+a for m in re.finditer(r'\n(?=\\(?:subsection|subsubsection|section|paragraph|fig|begin|end)\b)',s[a:])]
    if boundaries: b=min(b,*boundaries)
    if b<0: raise ValueError(start)
    old=s[a:b]
    # A paragraph replacement must not swallow figures, equations or tables.
    assert not any(x in old for x in [r'\begin{table}',r'\begin{equation}',r'\begin{align}',r'\fig{']), start
    s=s[:a]+new+s[b:]
    changes.append(start)

paragraph('Class imbalance occurs',r'''Class imbalance leaves a classifier with relatively few examples of the minority class. Resampling can improve its representation, but may also introduce noise or remove useful information. We investigate Coverage-Aware Safe-Border SMOTE (CASB), which distributes a fixed generation budget across overlapping pools of all minority observations, supported borderline observations and sparse minority observations. The four variants use conditionally capped interpolation, unrestricted interpolation, unrestricted interpolation followed by majority-only edited nearest neighbours, and source duplication. We evaluated them on 44 binary KEEL tasks using the five supplied folds, two seeds and training-only ratio selection, with SVM, KNN, random forest and logistic regression as classifiers. Forty tasks support complete comparisons with SMOTE, Borderline-SMOTE, random oversampling and ADASYN; ADASYN failed in 52 evaluations across the four classifiers. For random forest, the retrospective CASB family exceeded the four core baselines on G-mean in 28 of 40 tasks and on F1 in 26, although the selected variant could differ by metric and task. Some fixed-variant gains remained significant after classifier-specific Holm correction, alongside losses in precision. No paired Wilcoxon comparison survived correction in the source-family sensitivity analyses. A separate Gaussian overlap experiment and audited generation examples examined the mechanisms behind these outcomes. The results support a reproducible resampling design whose benefits depend on the classifier and metric. Matched cleaner ablations and evaluation on independent source families are needed before broader claims of superiority can be made.''')
paragraph('Machine learning is a branch',r'''In supervised classification, a model learns the relationship between input features and class labels. When one class is much smaller than another, its observations may be sparse or isolated, including in regions shared with the majority class. A dataset with 95 percent class A observations and 5 percent class B observations illustrates the problem: predicting class A for every case gives 95\% accuracy and zero minority recall. Accuracy alone therefore gives a misleading account of minority detection.''')
paragraph('Oversampling techniques  helps',r'''Oversampling increases the minority class representation in the training set. Random oversampling (ROS) duplicates existing minority observations, while SMOTE creates a point between a minority source and one of its minority neighbours \citep{chawla2002}. Both can change the learned decision boundary, but the added observations are not independent evidence. A synthetic point inherits its source label even when the line between its endpoints crosses an overlapping region or connects disconnected components. Duplication avoids this geometric assumption, although it increases the influence of the observations being copied.''')
paragraph('This study examines',r'''We study how to distribute a fixed number of additions among different minority observations. CASB draws sources from three overlapping pools: coverage contains all minority observations; border contains observations in locally mixed neighbourhoods with minority support; and rare contains sparse observations with minority support. Original CASB limits some interpolation steps according to the distance from the source to the nearest observed majority point. The other variants remove these limits, clean majority observations after generation, or duplicate selected sources. Comparing these configurations helps distinguish the roles of source allocation, generation and subsequent cleaning.''')
paragraph('Limiting the interpolation',r'''Restricting interpolation does not necessarily improve every metric. Removing majority observations can also increase minority recall while reducing precision. We report G-mean, F1, precision and recall separately to retain these tradeoffs.''')
paragraph('The contribution is an implementation',r'''We specify and implement four CASB variants, audit their generation and fallback rules, and evaluate them with explicit accounting for failures and selection decisions. The analysis includes fixed variants and retrospective family summaries. Confusion matrices describe changes in classification errors, while sensitivity analyses examine the statistical conclusions. The study does not establish that this exact combination is the first of its kind.''')
paragraph('Random Oversampling will',r'''Random oversampling increases the weight of minority observations by repeating them. It provides a useful baseline because it makes no assumptions about where a new minority point should lie. Its limitation is that it adds no new feature values and can amplify an incorrect label through repeated copies. Despite its simplicity, ROS can outperform more elaborate methods and remains an important comparator.''')
paragraph('SMOTE synthesises',r'''SMOTE generates minority samples by interpolating between nearby minority observations (Chawla et al., 2002). The generator is simple, but basic SMOTE does not use surrounding majority labels to constrain the selected segment. Even a short segment can cross a majority region when its endpoints lie on opposite sides of that region.''')
paragraph('Borderline-SMOTE focuses',r'''Borderline-SMOTE concentrates generation around minority observations identified as being in danger from their local labels \citep{han2005}. This directs additions towards difficult boundaries instead of distributing them uniformly. We use borderline-1. Its source-selection rule excludes other minority observations from direct allocation; whether broader allocation is preferable remains an empirical question. CASB reserves part of its budget for the all-minority pool to examine this tradeoff.''')
paragraph('ADASYN focuses',r'''ADASYN assigns more additions to minority observations with greater local learning difficulty \citep{he2008}. Its allocation adapts to the data, whereas CASB uses fixed branch weights. A high local majority fraction can indicate either a useful boundary observation or an unreliable minority label; the count alone does not distinguish them. The ADASYN failures in this study reflect feasibility problems in the executed procedure. We exclude incomplete tasks from every method in the matched comparison.''')
paragraph('The Safe-Level-SMOTE',r'''Safe-Level-SMOTE uses local minority support to modify interpolation \citep{safe2009}. Geometric SMOTE samples from a configurable geometric region rather than the original SMOTE line segment \citep{geometric2019}. CASB also uses local information, but limits displacement according to the allocated branch and nearest-majority distance. Some coverage steps remain unrestricted. A distance limit cannot guarantee the label of a generated point, especially when the source label is wrong.''')
paragraph('Density based methods',r'''Density-based methods use structure within the minority class. DBSMOTE uses density information to generate points \citep{dbsmote}, and K-means/SMOTE allocates additions using clusters \citep{kmeans}. CASB uses a simpler sparsity measure: the distance to the third nearest minority neighbour, thresholded at the 75th percentile. It does not fit clusters or identify verified minority subpopulations. This distance also depends on the feature representation and may become less informative in high-dimensional spaces.''')
paragraph('I find that categorizing',r'''Minority-instance classification is a close predecessor to CASB's source selection. \citet{napierala2016} distinguish borderline, rare and outlier observations using local class structure, while \citet{skryjomski2017} examine how minority-instance types affect SMOTE. Local Neighborhood Encodings (LNE) optimize resampling rates for neighbourhood types using both oversampling and undersampling \citep{lne2024}. CASB combines overlapping pools with fixed weights, so its contribution does not lie in the general idea of selecting minority-instance types. LNE is not included in this benchmark, and the results cannot establish an advantage over it.''')
paragraph('The edited nearest neighbour',r'''Edited nearest neighbours (ENN) removes observations whose labels disagree with their neighbours. We use the imbalanced-learn implementation, with explicit choices of the targeted class and voting rule (cite). Cleaning after generation can alter the training set substantially because synthetic observations participate in the neighbour votes. CASB-edited uses an established cleaner and makes no claim to a new ENN rule.''')
paragraph('While the classic comparison',r'''A general SMOTE-ENN comparison does not provide a matched control for CASB-edited. Our cleaner targets the majority class and uses three-neighbour mode voting; other hybrid implementations can target different classes or use different settings. The current comparison therefore cannot isolate the benefit of CASB generation. A direct control would apply exactly the same majority-only cleaner after SMOTE.''')
paragraph('UGO uses uncertainty',r'''UGO selects sources using classifier uncertainty, repeatedly refits the classifier, and filters or relocates generated observations (cite). It supports different generators within the same framework. CASB also separates allocation from generation, but computes its neighbourhood-based allocation once. Similarity in this organisation does not establish comparable runtime or predictive performance. We did not include UGO results in the CASB comparison tables.''')
paragraph('D is the set containing',r'''Let \(\mathcal{D}=\{(x_i,y_i)\}_{i=1}^{n}\) denote the preprocessed training set, where each \(x_i\) has \(p\) features and each observation has one of two class labels. The minority class is assigned label \(1\). The index sets \(M\) and \(J\) contain minority and majority observations, with sizes \(n_m\) and \(n_j\), respectively. Numeric features are median-imputed and scaled using training-data estimates only. Distances in this representation are Euclidean: the distance between \(x\) and \(x'\) is the \(L_2\) norm of \(x-x'\).''')
