paragraph('Within each branch, sources',r'''Sources are sampled uniformly with replacement within each branch. If $B$ or $R$ is empty, source selection falls back to $C$ while retaining the allocated branch's neighbour and interpolation rules. A rare-branch fallback therefore still uses at most the nearest two minority neighbours and the rare margin.''')
paragraph('Its expected number of uses',r'''The expected number of uses of source $i$ is $G\pi_i$. Every minority point is eligible through coverage, but some may never be selected: that probability is $\prod_b(1-\mathbf{1}(i\in P_b)/|P_b|)^{a_b}$. Membership in several pools increases a source's expected influence. The source-mass bubbles in the audited illustration represent these quantities.'''.split('\n')[0]) if False else None
# Keep adjacent figure calls intact when changing a standalone prose line.
def line(start,new):
    global s
    a=s.index(start);b=s.index('\n',a);s=s[:a]+new+s[b:];changes.append(start)
line('Its expected number of uses',r'''The expected number of uses of source $i$ is $G\pi_i$. Every minority point is eligible through coverage, but some may never be selected: that probability is $\prod_b(1-\mathbf{1}(i\in P_b)/|P_b|)^{a_b}$. Membership in several pools increases a source's expected influence. The source-mass bubbles in the audited illustration represent these quantities.''')
line('Thus $z$ is at least',r'''The triangle inequality therefore places $z$ at least as close to its source as to any observed majority point. This geometric property does not identify the true class of $z$, and it does not apply to unrestricted coverage steps or relaxed/edited generation. Unobserved majority points and incorrect source labels remain possible.''')
paragraph(r'\paragraph{Original CASB.}',r'''\paragraph{Original CASB.} Original CASB uses 50/30/20 quotas and conditional interpolation limits. It allocates part of the budget to locally mixed and sparse regions while aiming to keep selected steps conservative. The word ``safe'' describes this aim, not a guarantee of correct labels.''')
paragraph('Relaxed, 70/20/10 ratios.',r'''Relaxed uses 70/20/10 quotas and sets $\lambda_{\max}=1$ for every nonzero edge. Pool definitions, fallback rules and rare-neighbour restrictions remain unchanged. Because it increases the coverage allocation and removes interpolation limits at the same time, comparing it with original CASB does not isolate either change.''')
paragraph('Edited method starts',r'''Edited first applies relaxed generation, then runs ENN on the original majority label using three-neighbour mode voting. A targeted point is removed if the local mode disagrees with its label. All original and generated minority points are retained. If $h$ majority observations are removed, the resulting ratio is $(n_m+G)/(n_j-h)$, which may exceed the requested ratio. The implementation raises an error if fewer than two majority observations remain. This cleaner cannot remove mislabeled minority observations.''')
line('CASB-ROS, original source',r'''CASB-ROS retains the original source pools and 50/30/20 quotas but sets $z=x_s$. It changes the multiplicity of selected sources without creating new coordinates. Ordinary ROS does not use CASB's pool-conditioned source probabilities. The implementation still draws a neighbour and interpolation value before setting the actual step to zero, preserving the random sequence structure. This variant examines guided repetition, although differences in selected ratios and rounding prevent it from being a complete ablation of interpolation.''')
paragraph('Nearest-neighbour structures',r'''The implementation fits nearest-neighbour structures to the full training set and to each class separately. With brute-force search, the required neighbourhoods cost up to $O(n_mnp)$ distance arithmetic, plus backend-dependent neighbour selection. Generation costs $O(Gp)$ once the neighbour lists are stored. ENN requires a further search on the augmented data, with a brute-force upper bound of $O((n+G)^2p)$. Tree-based searches may reduce this cost in some low-dimensional settings. Stored features and output require $O((n+G)p)$ memory, and audit records add $O(G)$ entries; temporary memory depends on library batching.''')
line('Per-addition audits record',r'''Each addition has an audit record containing its source, neighbour, overlap, support, majority distance, branch and interpolation bounds. These records allow direct checks of $z=x_s+\lambda(x_t-x_s)$. We did not conduct a controlled runtime benchmark.''')
paragraph('The current four-classifier benchmark',r'''The four-classifier benchmark contains $44\times5\times2\times9\times4=15{,}840$ attempted outer evaluations: 15,788 succeeded and 52 failed. The earlier SVM/KNN-only guide reported 7,920 attempts and 26 failures, which remain valid for that subset. Including auxiliary Gaussian naive Bayes runs and compatible historical RUS/SMOTE-ENN results brings the statistical archive to 21,560 attempts and 65 failures. These archive totals describe a broader collection than the current nine-method benchmark.''')
line('Related tasks share source',r'''Several tasks share an original dataset. Glass, Yeast, Ecoli and Vehicle variants, for example, are not independent acquisition studies. The sensitivity analysis groups the tasks into 14 source families, or 12 for complete ADASYN comparisons. All 44 tasks have contributed to development or analysis, including the 38 added after the initial six; they do not form an untouched confirmation set. Table~\ref{tab:datasets} lists the inventory, and the evidence package retains the individual task scores.''')
paragraph('RF and LR were retained',r'''We retained RF and LR in the main presentation after inspecting their results. Gaussian naive Bayes was also evaluated and is reported as an auxiliary result. This choice makes the four-classifier presentation exploratory. A confirmatory study would need to specify its classifier set before inspecting outcomes.''')
paragraph('Every outer training fold',r'''Each outer training fold is divided into an inner training set and a stratified 25\% validation set. Every sampler tries ratios .50, .75 and 1.00. We select the ratio that maximizes $(G\text{-mean}+F1)/2$ on validation data, preferring the smaller ratio when scores tie. This objective is used only for ratio selection; the reported outcomes remain separate metrics. Imputation and scaling are refitted for every inner fit. We then refit the selected pipeline on the full outer training fold and evaluate it on untouched outer test data.''')
paragraph('The two Abalone tasks',r'''The two Abalone tasks include a categorical Sex attribute. We apply most-frequent imputation and dense one-hot encoding fitted on training data, ignoring unknown test categories. Numeric features use the usual numeric pipeline. Interpolation in this encoded space can produce fractional dummy values. The generator is therefore not categorical-aware, and comparison with SMOTENC remains future work.''')
paragraph('Two different retrospective quantities',r'''We report two retrospective summaries. The collection envelope, $\max_{a\in\mathcal F}A_{cam}$, chooses one family member for an entire metric column. Dataset-wise family wins count $\max_{a\in\mathcal F}S_{dcam}>\max_{b\in\mathcal B}S_{dcbm}$ and can select a different variant for each dataset. Both use observed outcomes and therefore cannot serve as deployable selectors or confirmatory paired tests. A joint G-mean/F1 win requires the same variant to exceed every baseline on both metrics. Ties use tolerance $10^{-12}$.''')
paragraph('Friedman tests use',r'''Friedman tests use the strict 40-task cohort, with eleven methods for SVM/KNN and nine for RF/LR. We apply Holm correction to the four omnibus tests within each classifier; post-hoc all-pairs comparisons form a separate correction family. Sensitivity analyses use source-family mean differences and one lexicographically selected task per family. We also retain exact sign tests. These analyses examine how conclusions change with the test and unit of comparison, but cannot undo classifier selection or reuse of development data.''')
line(r'Figures~\ref{fig:noise}',r'''Figures~\ref{fig:noise} and \ref{fig:controlled} show sampler outputs on fixed constructed examples. The coverage pool allows CASB to generate from a deliberately mislabeled minority source. Its cap restricts movement in some branches without detecting that error. Curved minority regions also illustrate how a straight segment between nearby minority endpoints can leave the minority support. These examples expose failure modes alongside the intended behaviour.''')
paragraph(r'Figures~\ref{fig:iam}',r'''Figures~\ref{fig:iam}--\ref{fig:recall} use the same axes and four classifier panels. Each point averages twenty repeats, with a pointwise 95\% Student-$t$ interval. The bands describe variation at each distance; they are neither simultaneous confidence bands nor paired significance tests. At small $d$, resampling cannot supply information that separates identical class-conditional distributions. At large $d$, a sampler's interpolation procedure may become infeasible. Missing ADASYN cells indicate incomplete evaluations, not zero scores.''')
line(r'Table~\ref{tab:simulation_comparison} summarizes',r'''Table~\ref{tab:simulation_comparison} averages the complete distance range with equal distance and seed weights for every method. This simulation represents one distributional family and one imbalance ratio, so we keep its averages separate from real-task scores. For inference, the simulation notebook first averages distances within each seed, leaving twenty independent units. The 220 seed-distance combinations are correlated and are not treated as independent datasets.''')
paragraph(r'Table~\ref{tab:benchmark_comparison} groups',r'''All methods in Table~\ref{tab:benchmark_comparison} use the same forty datasets. The RF results illustrate the tradeoff: CASB-edited achieves G-mean 79.320\% and recall 73.502\%, while CASB-ROS achieves precision 72.014\%. Relaxed has the highest family RF F1 at 67.855\%, slightly below SMOTE's 67.907\%. Winning on many datasets does not necessarily give a method the highest collection average on every metric.''')
line('For SVM, CASB-ROS',r'''For SVM, CASB-ROS has the highest family F1 average, while edited leads on G-mean and recall. Original CASB leads family F1 and precision for KNN. LR shows small family-average advantages over the core resamplers, with no basis for treating them as universally decisive. The no-resampling reference can retain high precision while missing many minority cases, so its precision must be considered together with recall.''')
paragraph(r'Table~\ref{tab:envelope} selects',r'''Table~\ref{tab:envelope} compares the highest collection-wide family average with the highest core-baseline average for each metric. Table~\ref{tab:family} instead selects within each dataset. Against the core baselines, RF has 28/40 family wins on G-mean and 26/40 on F1, with 20/40 same-variant joint wins. Including all available baselines changes these counts to 27, 26 and 19. Even RF therefore lacks a strict majority of joint wins against the expanded baseline set.''')
line("SVM and KNN gain a stronger",r'''Compatible SMOTE-ENN results add a stronger G-mean/recall comparator for SVM and KNN. SVM's best family-average G-mean is below SMOTE-ENN, while KNN edited exceeds it slightly. Including no resampling also changes the precision envelope. The core-four comparison addresses the original research question, but it cannot support a claim against all established alternatives.''')
paragraph('All sixteen four-classifier Friedman',r'''All sixteen Friedman tests reject after within-classifier correction (Table~\ref{tab:friedman}), indicating differences within each method set. They do not identify CASB as the superior method. Fixed-variant pairwise comparisons show both improvements and losses (Table~\ref{tab:significance}); Figure~\ref{fig:significance} displays their direction.''')
line('The source-family analysis materially',r'''The source-family analysis weakens the dataset-level findings. No Wilcoxon comparison survives Holm correction after paired differences are averaged within source families, and none survives in the one-task-per-family analysis. With fewer independent units and substantial heterogeneity, these results do not establish equivalence, but they do limit claims of broad superiority. Nine corrected source-family exact-sign comparisons reject. The sign and Wilcoxon tests address different properties, so both results are retained alongside the declared primary analysis.''')
paragraph('The historical selector chooses',r'''The historical selector uses saved inner-validation scores to choose among original, relaxed and edited CASB at three ratios. CASB-ROS was not a candidate. Because outer scores do not enter this decision, the corresponding saved outer refit can be reused for that recorded choice. The procedure was nevertheless specified retrospectively on development data. Table~\ref{tab:selector} reports SVM/KNN metrics over all 44 tasks: equal-classifier G-mean is 83.066\% and F1 is 68.001\%. These cannot be compared directly with the forty-task envelope. The selector also has nine inner trials, compared with three for a fixed baseline. We infer no RF/LR selector performance.''')
line('Gaussian naive Bayes provides',r'''Gaussian naive Bayes offers an additional check on selective presentation. On the forty-task cohort, edited achieves G-mean 68.310\% and F1 46.971\%, compared with SMOTE's 67.699\% and 46.142\%. Its recall is 85.216\%, below ADASYN's 86.790\%. We retain these results even though GNB is absent from the main figure panels. Future studies should specify the full classifier set before evaluation.''')
paragraph('Original CASB limits displacement',r'''The variants change distinct parts of the training procedure. Original CASB limits displacement in capped branches; relaxed changes both quotas and displacement; edited adds majority-only removal; and CASB-ROS changes multiplicities without adding coordinates. These are implementation properties, independent of any measured gain. Original CASB does not filter noise, and edited does not clean minority labels.''')
paragraph(r'Table~\ref{tab:mechanisms} uses',r'''Table~\ref{tab:mechanisms} compares edited with original CASB using recorded confusion matrices. False-positive and false-negative rates are computed per fold, then averaged within each dataset and across the forty matched tasks. This gives each dataset equal influence instead of pooling counts dominated by large test sets. Saved diagnostics also provide majority-removal percentages, using the original majority training count as denominator.''')
paragraph('The restricted generator can produce',r'''Restricting generation can concentrate additions near their sources and may help preserve precision by limiting their influence farther away. This could explain original CASB's relative precision/F1 strength, but the current results do not establish causation or show that original CASB always has fewer false positives than SMOTE. Testing that explanation requires a cap-only ablation with the same quotas and ratio.''')
paragraph('KNN responds directly',r'''KNN depends on nearby labels and distances: copies can change neighbourhood composition, while new coordinates can change which points are nearest. For an RBF SVM, duplicate weighting and interpolation can affect kernel similarities and the regularized margin differently. RF responds through its splits and leaf class proportions, and LR through its fitted linear score and empirical loss. These model properties make classifier-dependent effects plausible. The benchmark does not establish a general causal relationship between each source type and classifier.''')
paragraph('CASB-ROS gains over ROS',r'''CASB-ROS gains over ROS are consistent with a benefit from guided source weighting, although Table~\ref{tab:paired} also records losses. They do not establish that interpolation is unnecessary. Likewise, edited recall gains cannot demonstrate minority-noise removal because every minority point is retained.''')
paragraph(r'Figures~\ref{fig:pca_haberman}',r'''The real-data PCA examples in Figures~\ref{fig:pca_haberman}--\ref{fig:pca_yeast} were selected after evaluation for favourable SVM/KNN family G-mean differences. They are positive case studies. Generation takes place in the full transformed feature space; PCA is fitted on original training data and then applied to all panels. Each plot uses fold 1 and seed 42 with the method's saved SVM-selected ratio, so sample counts can differ across methods.''')
line('PCA may conceal',r'''PCA can hide separation present in higher dimensions. CASB and SMOTE can produce similar projected clouds because they share an interpolation rule; CASB changes source allocation and some step lengths. Provenance identifies the added observations. Visual similarity or separation in the projection does not establish label validity or predictive improvement.''')
paragraph('The available variants are informative',r'''The variants compare useful configurations, but do not form a complete factorial ablation. Original versus relaxed changes both quotas and caps. Edited versus relaxed can change the selected ratio as well as add cleaning. CASB versus CASB-ROS changes coordinates and may also change the selected ratio. A controlled study should fix ratios and source draws while varying coverage-only, border and rare allocation, caps and cleaning separately. The most immediate missing control is SMOTE followed by the same majority-only three-neighbour mode cleaner.''')
paragraph('Neighbourhood size, overlap thresholds',r'''We did not exhaustively assess neighbourhood size, overlap thresholds, the sparsity quantile or margins. Their effects may depend on dimensionality, scaling, duplicates and minority sample size. In particular, third-neighbour distance may assign similar sparsity to a legitimate minority component and a mislabeled observation. Support and positive-distance checks restrict some branches, but leave the all-minority pool unprotected. A label-noise study would need declared training-label corruption rates and clean independent test data; the current overlap experiment does not provide that evidence.''')
paragraph('The benchmark uses supplied',r'''The benchmark reuses development data and contains related tasks evaluated with supplied folds and two seeds. Identical feature vectors may occur across supplied partitions, so a group-disjoint sensitivity analysis is still needed. Repeated seeds describe stochastic variation rather than new populations. The lack of corrected source-family Wilcoxon findings limits the current evidence and motivates testing on independent data; it does not imply that a future test will become significant.''')
paragraph('Baseline coverage is another',r'''The baseline set limits the scope of the comparison. The results concern familiar resamplers and do not establish superiority over Safe-Level-SMOTE, Geometric SMOTE, LNE, density-aware methods or UGO, which were not benchmarked. Compatible historical RUS/SMOTE-ENN results extend the SVM/KNN comparison, but matched RF/LR controls are unavailable. Further methods should receive the same preprocessing, classifier settings and selection budgets.''')
paragraph('CASB combines broad',r'''CASB distributes additions across all minority observations and supported borderline and sparse sources. Its four variants use conditionally capped interpolation, unrestricted interpolation, majority-edited interpolation and guided replication. The specified rules and audits make the procedure reproducible, while the displacement bound offers only a geometric constraint, not a label or generalization guarantee.''')
paragraph('The forty-task matched benchmark',r'''The forty-task benchmark shows benefits that depend on the classifier and metric. For RF, the retrospective family wins on G-mean in 28 tasks and F1 in 26 against the four core resamplers, with twenty same-variant joint wins. Its best mean F1 remains slightly below SMOTE. Some fixed-variant gains survive correction, but precision losses and the absence of corrected source-family Wilcoxon findings limit the case for broader superiority. The simulation and projected examples explain aspects of generation without establishing robustness to label noise.''')
paragraph('Future work requires',r'''Further evaluation should use matched component controls, direct comparisons with close predecessors, categorical-aware generation, and a frozen selection procedure tested on new source families. The present contribution is an incremental resampling design with reproducible rules and measured tradeoffs.''')

